# NestJS bootstrap (main.ts) that never registers response security headers.
# NestJS adds NO security headers by default — the app must opt in during
# bootstrap, right after NestFactory.create(), via app.useSecurityHeaders()
# (NestJS >= v12.1) or app.use(helmet()). A main.ts that wires up the app but
# registers none of these ships every response without CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options,
# X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy or the X-Powered-By strip — so the API
# is open to clickjacking, MIME-sniffing and TLS-downgrade attacks.
# Sourced from docs/research/nestjs.md antipattern #1 / best-practice #1
# (NestJS Helmet docs: https://docs.nestjs.com/security/helmet).
#
# HOW (absence engine): fires when the bootstrap file contains NONE of the
# header-registration markers (the helmet call, the built-in security-headers
# call, the powered-by hider, or a literal security-header name). Targeted at
# main.ts by filename so it only judges the bootstrap entrypoint, not arbitrary
# code. absence engine => the satisfying pattern absent means "fire".
id: KEUR-NEST-002
maturity: 1
title: NestJS main.ts registers no response security headers
category: security
severity: warning
engine: absence
action: warn
target:
  filenames: main.ts
patterns:
  # satisfied (no finding) if the bootstrap registers security headers by any
  # recognised means, OR names a specific security header. Absent => fire.
  - regex: (?i)\bhelmet\b|useSecurityHeaders|hidePoweredBy|disable\s*\(\s*['"]x-powered-by['"]|Strict-Transport-Security|Content-Security-Policy|X-Frame-Options|X-Content-Type-Options|Referrer-Policy|Permissions-Policy
    message: "NestJS antipattern #1 — main.ts bootstrap registers no response security headers. NestJS adds none by default, so every response ships without CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy or Permissions-Policy. Call app.useSecurityHeaders() (or app.use(helmet())) immediately after NestFactory.create()."
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Right after NestFactory.create(), call app.useSecurityHeaders() (NestJS >= v12.1) or app.use(helmet()) before any other app.use()/route middleware, so the default CSP + HSTS + nosniff + frame + powered-by-strip headers cover every response."
